The approach is to use the fact that the random generator repeats, then precompute each section once and print it in large blocks.

**Why the generator repeats.** The LCG uses modulus m = 139968 = 2⁶·3⁷, multiplier a = 3877 and increment c = 29573. These satisfy the Hull–Dobell conditions:
- c is coprime to m (it is odd and its digit sum, 26, is not divisible by 3).
- a − 1 = 3876 = 4·3·323 is divisible by 4 and by 3.

So the seed sequence has full period P = 139968.

**Building the cycle strings.** Starting from seed 42, walk all P seeds once. For each seed, record the IUB symbol and the HomoSapiens symbol. The cumulative probabilities are summed in table order, matching the reference float behaviour. This gives one cycle string per table.
- Section TWO reads its table's cycle starting at offset 0.
- Section THREE reads its table's cycle starting at offset 3N mod P, so the seed continues from where section TWO ended.

**Wrapping without per-line work.** Each sequence is a repeating source string of period p, split into 60-column lines.
- The pair (position in source, position in line) repeats every lcm(p, 60) characters: 699840 for the random cycles and 17220 for ALU.
- Each section precomputes one wrapped block of that length with `chunk_split`.
- It writes that block as many times as needed, then wraps the leftover tail.
- Because the block length is a multiple of 60, line alignment stays correct.
- `chunk_split` also ends the last line with a newline even when it is shorter than 60 characters.

```php
<?php
$n = (int)$argv[1];
$out = fopen('php://stdout', 'w');

function gcd($a, $b) { while ($b) { $t = $a % $b; $a = $b; $b = $t; } return $a; }

function emit($out, $cyc, $start, $len) {
    if ($len <= 0) return;
    $P = strlen($cyc);
    $B = intdiv($P * 60, gcd($P, 60));
    $reps = intdiv($start + $B, $P) + 2;
    $sub = substr(str_repeat($cyc, $reps), $start, $B);
    $full = intdiv($len, $B);
    $rem = $len % $B;
    if ($full > 0) {
        $block = chunk_split($sub, 60, "\n");
        // group several blocks for fewer writes
        $group = max(1, intdiv(4000000, strlen($block)));
        $big = str_repeat($block, $group);
        $f = $full;
        while ($f >= $group) { fwrite($out, $big); $f -= $group; }
        while ($f > 0) { fwrite($out, $block); $f--; }
        unset($big, $block);
    }
    if ($rem > 0) {
        fwrite($out, chunk_split(substr($sub, 0, $rem), 60, "\n"));
    }
}

