The program follows the classic benchmarks-game approach: read all of stdin, then process one record at a time.

1. Read the whole input with `stream_get_contents`.
2. Locate each record by the next `"\n>"`.
3. For each record:
   - Print the header line unchanged.
   - Strip newlines (and `\r`) from the sequence body.
   - Reverse the body with `strrev`.
   - Complement it with a single `strtr` lookup table that covers both upper and lower case and outputs upper case.
4. Re-wrap the result at 60 columns with `chunk_split`.

The 60-column width is assumed from the standard benchmark output; the program does not measure the input line length. An empty sequence prints only its header. `memory_limit` is lifted because large inputs are held in memory.

```php
<?php
ini_set('memory_limit', '-1');

$n = isset($argv[1]) ? (int)$argv[1] : 0;

$data = stream_get_contents(STDIN);
$len = strlen($data);

$from = "ACGTUMRWSYKVHDBNacgtumrwsykvhdbn";
$to   = "TGCAAKYWSRMBDHVNTGCAAKYWSRMBDHVN";

$out = fopen('php://stdout', 'w');
stream_set_write_buffer($out, 1 << 20);

$pos = strpos($data, '>');
while ($pos !== false && $pos < $len) {
    $hdrEnd = strpos($data, "\n", $pos);
    if ($hdrEnd === false) {
        fwrite($out, substr($data, $pos) . "\n");
        break;
    }
    $header = substr($data, $pos, $hdrEnd - $pos);
    $next = strpos($data, "\n>", $hdrEnd);
    if ($next === false) {
        $seqEnd = $len;
        $nextPos = false;
    } else {
        $seqEnd = $next;
        $nextPos = $next + 1;
    }
    $seq = substr($data, $hdrEnd + 1, $seqEnd - $hdrEnd - 1);
    $seq = str_replace(["\n", "\r"], '', $seq);
    fwrite($out, $header . "\n");
    if ($seq !== '') {
        $seq = strtr(strrev($seq), $from, $to);
        fwrite($out, chunk_split($seq, 60, "\n"));
    }
    unset($seq);
    $pos = $nextPos;
}
fclose($out);
```

